docs/typescript-settings.md:118:Imported core files can appear in more than one project's graph. A failure may therefore reveal a real environment assumption rather than a duplicated source file. `saved-format.ts`, for example, uses JSON and text encoding without reading the DOM, allowing the same validators to serve both browser and Node clients.
docs/board-code-format.md:41:The initial deck has `N * N` positions, numbered from zero in row order. Before shuffling it is built with `Math.floor(index / 2)`, giving `[0, 0, 1, 1, ...]`. There are `N * N / 2` faces, and every face appears twice. Shuffling swaps positions; it does not change the face values or duplicate counts.
docs/import-safety-policy.md:43:The helpers operate on the parsed object, not the original token stream. JSON duplicate property names are consequently handled by `JSON.parse` before exact-key checking; the decoder does not retain a record of earlier overwritten occurrences. Likewise, numeric notation such as `1.0` becomes the number `1`. The integer predicate checks the value it receives rather than enforcing a preferred spelling of a JSON number.
docs/persistence-envelope.md:31:`src/game/tournament/storage.ts` reads and writes `{ v: 3, items: [...] }` at `np3:tournaments`. Each item is a validated completed `TournamentState`; invalid or unfinished records reject the stored collection. The reader keeps the newest ten unique events. This code does not use `src/game/session/storage.ts`: `readPreferences` expects the existing aggregate, and feeding it a feature-shaped collection returns default preferences. The two representations have different jobs and are not interchangeable encodings of the same object.
docs/theme-tokens.md:57:The green palette entry and `--green` have the same current hex value but separate declaration sites. An edit to the CSS state color alone does not recolor right-marker artwork. An edit to the TypeScript entry alone does not change matched borders. A reviewer can decide whether both changes were intended by examining their roles, rather than assuming the duplicate literal is accidental.
docs/win-conditions.md:33:The count comparison assumes valid state. A hand-constructed array with duplicated positions could have the correct length without representing a completed board. Normal activation prevents duplicates by ignoring matched positions. Snapshot decoding separately checks unique in-range indices and complete pairs before constructing a restored game.
docs/interface-review-checklist.md:82:- Face and check SVGs do not introduce extra focus targets or duplicate the button's description.
docs/interface-review-checklist.md:95:- Check normalization, the length message and a case-only duplicate before starting.
docs/retention-policy.md:11:The tournament history applies this rule on completion: `retainTournaments` orders by the ISO completion time, removes identical event records and keeps at most ten. It does not turn the existing `bestMoves` object into a history or add a timestamp to it. Record types, sort fields and tie-breaking conventions are separate from the amount retained.
docs/incident-snapshot-rejects.md:35:A duplicate such as `[0, 11, 0, 11]` is invalid, and `[0, 2]` is invalid because it contains halves of different pairs. Order is not the property that separates these cases. Uniqueness, range, and complete face counts are.
docs/announcements-policy.md:50:The tournament controller publishes `Now playing: NAME` when an event begins, a turn begins or a match advances. After a turn, it publishes the player's moves and formatted time with the opponent handoff, the match winner, or the champion. These messages share the active Tournament screen's status region; bracket rows remain readable result data and do not duplicate event announcements.
docs/icon-and-glyph-policy.md:55:A decorative SVG inside a card uses `aria-hidden="true"` and `focusable="false"`. The button supplies its own accessible name. Giving the internal SVG a separate title would change that accessibility structure; the current renderer avoids duplicated face descriptions by naming the button once through `describeCard`.
docs/duplicate-names-policy.md:13:The check concerns names, rather than board positions or face identities. Two cards intentionally share a face, and a replay can intentionally activate the same position more than once. Those repetitions follow their own game rules. A generic “remove duplicates everywhere” helper would not express this policy's subject or scope.
docs/duplicate-names-policy.md:49:`src/game/tournament/rules.test.ts` checks case-insensitive duplicate rejection, and `tests/tournament.e2e.test.ts` submits a case-only repeat and checks the exact visible notice. A screenshot of distinct displayed names alone would not establish this behavior; submission is the relevant boundary.
