docs/source-audit-notes.md:86:A file-length failure names the file. An export failure names the unconsumed symbol or duplicate owner. A predicate failure names the function. The environment test gives a changed set of path/name pairs. These failures are most useful when read alongside the small regex that produced them.
docs/tournament-mode.md:30:Completed tournaments are saved locally under `np3:tournaments` in the version 3 `{ "v": 3, "items": [...] }` envelope. The list is ordered newest completion first and retains at most ten unique events. It appears on the Tournament screen after reload. If browser storage cannot be used, the screen shows `Progress won't be saved on this device.` and the event remains playable in memory.
docs/unit-test-guide.md:77:Other cases cover whole matched pairs, duplicate indices, out-of-range selections, selecting a matched position, extra keys, pending misses with equal faces, and insufficient moves for progress. `snapshot-selection.test.ts` isolates selection and matched-position validation so a nested-field failure can be understood separately from outer-object validation.
docs/adr-022-card-names.md:31:Short names such as “Card 7” would identify a button but give less spatial structure for board navigation. A separate accessible image description would duplicate the button's meaning and risk becoming an extra navigable object. Describing every card with its face from initial load would make the game a different task for different users.
docs/browser-test-guide.md:74:Shared label constants keep ordinary journeys readable, but literal contract cases protect the actual wording. Changing a control's constant can otherwise change the test lookup and production label together. The dedicated contract files are deliberate independent evidence, rather than duplicate stylistic preferences.
docs/error-display-guide.md:23:`SessionNotice` in `session.ts` owns the literal messages. The shared `notice` helper sets both `feedback` and `loadNotice` and advances announcement identity. The exact words therefore come from the session boundary that knows the operation failed, rather than being duplicated in each field's change handler.
docs/error-display-guide.md:75:Tournament entry has separate name errors: the entry notice shows the length or duplicate-name message, and the Start tournament button is described by that notice. Tournament JSON uses its own load outcome beside the textarea. These messages do not replace or alter the solo game's load notices.
docs/data-exchange-policy.md:36:A snapshot carries `boardCode`, matched position indices, a tagged selection, moves, elapsed milliseconds and the `started` flag, alongside its format version. It does not duplicate the deck. Pair count and completion are derived after loading. Omitting those derived values avoids accepting a record in which the supplied pair total disagrees with the matched positions. Snapshot format gives the complete field and validation reference.
docs/README.md:78:- duplicate-names-policy.md: Group-scoped name comparison, case handling and evidence for repeat detection.
docs/randomness-policy.md:43:For a valid seed, the generator advances that sequence inside a descending Fisher–Yates shuffle. The initial deck contains two copies of every integer face from zero to `size ** 2 / 2 - 1`, arranged as adjacent duplicates before shuffling. For a 4 by 4 board, it starts as `0, 0, 1, 1` and so on through face 7.
docs/snapshot-selection-notes.md:78:This is useful when reviewing a reported missing validation. The code does not contain a literal `isWon` check in this module, but completion with a revealed unmatched selection is still impossible after a valid full matched list. Adding a duplicate branch might make the invariant easier to see, yet its absence is not by itself a bug.
docs/snapshot-selection-notes.md:84:`snapshot-selection.test.ts` tests extra keys on all three variants, remaining-time endpoints and a fractional interval, plus half-pair and duplicate-position failures. `snapshot.test.ts` reaches these helpers through the full decoder and adds matched/selected overlap, same-face pending pairs, invalid bounds and counter combinations.
docs/release-notes-1-3.md:19:It was tempting to think that recording the seed was enough. It is enough only when every consumer uses the same generator, initial deck ordering, draw mapping, and swap order. The current implementation begins with duplicated face IDs in ascending order, then shuffles backward from the final position.
docs/competition-pairing.md:50:This exercise reveals three different classes of error. A duplicated participant is a membership problem. Correct opponents in the wrong middle positions are an ordering problem. Correct seed positions attached to the wrong names are an entry-mapping problem. They can produce similar screenshots, but they point to different mistakes in construction.
docs/adr-025-reduced-motion.md:21:The browser preference is already a user-controlled signal and works for the offline file without storing a second application setting. A game-level toggle would have duplicated that preference and added another persistence question. Treating motion as required feedback would also have made the reduced presentation less informative.
docs/card-artwork.md:38:For a four-column board, index 6 is row 2, column 3. If it shows face 0, its name is `Row 2, column 3, revealed, cyan solid circle, marker top`. This example is derived directly from the helper, not from reading SVG geometry. The face and matched mark are both `aria-hidden`, so they do not duplicate that button name.
docs/snapshot-format.md:4:A snapshot is a position on a deterministic deal. It stores enough state to continue play without storing a duplicate deck or a sequence of earlier actions. `encodeSnapshot(state)` produces readable JSON; `decodeSnapshot(text)` returns an accepted `GameState` or `null`. Browser pause behavior is added after decoding by the session adapter, so it is not part of the JSON object.
docs/snapshot-format.md:81:Numeric sorting is significant on larger boards. Lexicographic string ordering would put position `10` before position `2`; the implementation explicitly uses numeric subtraction. Sorting is an export presentation choice, not a prerequisite for acceptance and not a way to repair duplicate or incomplete matches.
docs/snapshot-format.md:144:`hasExactKeys` combines an `Object.keys` count with `Object.hasOwn` for each required key. It does not care about enumeration order. At this string boundary the input has already been produced by `JSON.parse`; the helper is not a general schema engine or a tokenizer that remembers duplicate property occurrences. The same helpers serve replay parsing with different field lists and limits.
docs/regression-catalogue.md:60:Other parser regressions include duplicate matched positions, selecting a matched position, mismatches with equal faces and inconsistent move counts. `snapshot-selection.test.ts` and `snapshot.test.ts` cover those separately. The format validates state consistency, not a reconstructed history of every earlier reveal.

