docs/retention-policy.md:13:The list representation is discussed in Persistence envelope. Parsing and validation failures are covered in Corrupt data policy, because retention of accepted records and recovery from rejected input are different operations.
docs/retention-policy.md:19:The supported set has seven entries. Once read validation has succeeded, the score map can therefore contain at most seven different supported size keys. It can also contain fewer, including none. This bound follows from `GRID_SIZES` and canonical key validation, not from trimming an attempt history. Multiple completed games at the same size compete for the same map entry.
docs/retention-policy.md:25:## Why no clock drives current retention
docs/retention-policy.md:41:The tournament history saves after an event reaches its champion. It does not save an unfinished event on each turn or run an age-based cleanup timer. The preference effect remains separate and has no generic event log or timer that scans browser keys.
docs/retention-policy.md:45:The app has no account or service that copies preferences or tournament history elsewhere. A completed event remains local to that browser's storage context. Opening the same HTML in another browser does not fetch it. Tournament JSON can be exported manually; snapshots do not include tournament history, bests or selected-size settings.
docs/retention-policy.md:53:Conversely, a successful write does not prove indefinite lifetime. The browser can later clear data, a private context can end, or the person can remove site storage. Those environmental conditions are outside the saved-list retention operation. Private browsing notes describes how a value can survive a reload yet disappear when the browsing session ends.
docs/retention-policy.md:57:## Checking retention with feature records
docs/retention-policy.md:59:`src/game/tournament/storage.test.ts` checks unique ordering and the ten-item limit. `tests/tournament.e2e.test.ts` checks that a completed event survives a reload. `session/storage.test.ts` separately checks per-size best values and equal or worse results.
docs/retention-policy.md:61:For a feature-list review, concrete before-and-after examples can expose the retention boundary without adding fields to the product format. Records can be identified by the already defined domain representation in that feature's tests. The useful observation is which accepted records are present after a save, compared with the preceding accepted collection. Examples can use the feature's existing record fields.
docs/retention-policy.md:63:It is also useful to keep failed writes separate from successful retention checks. A denied store can leave the feature's in-memory state ahead of the browser value. Inspecting the old browser string then proves only that the write failed, not that the retention calculation chose the wrong records. Storage testing guide describes the store doubles that make this separation observable.
